{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013hMzwT5tgUHCjtfybDw9A8","type":"tool_result","content":"550:- **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct push gets patch, and ard's own main-lane commits (committer `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`) are skipped. The seed tag is `v0.1.0`. The workflow is a dry run until the repository variable `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. `make build` (`git describe` limited to plain `vX.Y.Z` tags) and `go install ...@vX.Y.Z` (build info) both print the tag from `ard version`. `internal/versioncheck` tests the workflow's scripts. See `docs/runbooks/release.md`.\n551:- **Release.** An annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` (`git tag -a`) builds that commit with `VERSION=vX.Y.Z`, pushes `:vX.Y.Z` and moves `:latest` when it is the highest version. Release tags are annotated because Jenkins builds only tags at most a day old and dates a lightweight tag by its commit, so a lightweight tag on an older commit never builds. Every merge to `main` is tagged by the Versioning step above; releases do not change what is deployed, which follows `main`.\n567:Phases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --cask albertcbrown/tap/ard`).\n57:deploy/test-*.sh            chart, image and repo checks for the above (`make chart-test`, `make image-test`); test-release.sh is `make release-check`\n58:.goreleaser.yaml            GoReleaser v2 config: four-target archives, checksums and the Homebrew cask (checked by `make release-check`)\n4:.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fix